<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hikone</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hikone is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a>. It grew around Hikone Castle, built by the Ii clan between 1603 and 1622, whose original keep is one of five in Japan designated National Treasures. As the castle town of the Ii, leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>, it was the seat of the Hikone domain until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hikone.jpg" alt="Hikone" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unmaokur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_hikone-jo_from_sawayama-jo_ruins.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hikone lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>, on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a>, and extends inland to the Ibuki Mountains. Parts of the city fall within the Biwako Quasi-National Park. The climate is humid subtropical, with warm summers and cool winters that bring light snowfall or none. The site was a natural bottleneck on the route of the Tōsandō, later the Nakasendō, the highway connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a> with the eastern provinces. Strategically it was a vital point for protecting the capital against attack from the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hikone was part of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a> and began as a market town around Hogon-ji, a Buddhist temple founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>. It has two former post stations on the Nakasendō, Toriimoto-juku and Takamiya-juku, and the Chōsenjin Kaidō also passed through the area. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> the Azai clan of northern Ōmi controlled it and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle">Sawayama Castle</a>. Oda Nobunaga defeated the Azai and gave the castle to his general Niwa Nagahide. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> it was entrusted to his close adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> appointed his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a> daimyō of a new domain centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle">Sawayama Castle</a>. Ii Naomasa began Hikone Castle, and his son Ii Naokatsu completed it in 1622. The area stayed under the Hikone Domain to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The town of Hikone was established on 1 April 1889 under the modern system of municipalities and became a city on 11 February 1937 by merging with neighbouring villages. It escaped major damage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hikone had an estimated 111,958 inhabitants in 2021. Its main industries are the manufacture of butsudan, textiles and valves. Hikone Castle is a National Treasure and Special National Historic Site, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle">Sawayama Castle</a> ruins and the Kōjinyama Kofun are also listed sites. The city has three universities and is the home of the Japan Center for Michigan Universities, founded in 1989. It is twinned with Ann Arbor, Michigan, since 1969, and with Xiangtan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hikone, Shiga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Matsumoto, Inuyama, Matsue and <b>Hikone</b> castles are also National Treasures, and Himeji's sister castles are the Château de Chantilly in France and Conwy Castle in Wales.</blockquote><a href="/work/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1602 his sons built <b>Hikone</b> castle nearby, completed in 1622.</blockquote><a href="/state/hikone-domain">Hikone Domain</a></li></ul></section>
